::ALG2_U04_L05_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Expand log<sub>2</sub>(xy). {
= log<sub>2</sub>(x) + log<sub>2</sub>(y)#Correct. The product property changes multiplication inside one logarithm into addition of logarithms.
~ log<sub>2</sub>(x) - log<sub>2</sub>(y)#Subtraction is used for quotients, not products.
~ log<sub>2</sub>(x + y)#This changes the expression and does not apply a log property.
~ 2log(xy)#This does not represent the product property.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Condense log<sub>5</sub>(a) - log<sub>5</sub>(b). {
~ log<sub>5</sub>(ab)#That would match addition of logarithms.
= log<sub>5</sub>(a/b)#Correct. The quotient property changes subtraction of same-base logarithms into division inside one logarithm.
~ log<sub>5</sub>(a - b)#There is no subtraction property inside a logarithm.
~ log<sub>5</sub>(b/a)#This reverses the quotient.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite 4log<sub>3</sub>(x) as one logarithm. {
~ log<sub>3</sub>(4x)#The coefficient becomes an exponent, not a factor.
~ log<sub>3</sub>(x + 4)#There is no addition property like this.
= log<sub>3</sub>(x<sup>4</sup>)#Correct. The power property moves the coefficient to an exponent inside the logarithm.
~ 4 + log<sub>3</sub>(x)#This is not equivalent to multiplying the logarithm by 4.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is equivalent to log<sub>7</sub>(m/n)? {
~ log<sub>7</sub>(m) + log<sub>7</sub>(n)#Addition corresponds to multiplication.
~ log<sub>7</sub>(m - n)#Subtraction inside a log does not split.
~ log<sub>7</sub>(n) - log<sub>7</sub>(m)#This reverses the quotient.
= log<sub>7</sub>(m) - log<sub>7</sub>(n)#Correct. The quotient property separates division into subtraction.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which rewrite is invalid? {
= log<sub>2</sub>(x + y) = log<sub>2</sub>(x) + log<sub>2</sub>(y)#Correct. Logarithms do not split over addition inside the argument.
~ log<sub>2</sub>(xy) = log<sub>2</sub>(x) + log<sub>2</sub>(y)#This is the product property.
~ log<sub>2</sub>(x/y) = log<sub>2</sub>(x) - log<sub>2</sub>(y)#This is the quotient property.
~ log<sub>2</sub>(x<sup>3</sup>) = 3log<sub>2</sub>(x)#This is the power property.
}

